<article class="env-lemma" id="000P">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/000P">Lemma <span data-tag="000P">3.9.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be an affine scheme. Let $R = \Gamma (S, \mathcal{O}_ S)$. Then the size of $S$ is equal to $\max \{  \aleph _0, |R|\} $. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      There are at most $\max \{ |R|, \aleph _0\} $ affine opens of $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$. This is clear since any affine open $U \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ is a finite union of principal opens $D(f_1) \cup \ldots \cup D(f_ n)$ and hence the number of affine opens is at most $\sup _ n |R|^ n = \max \{ |R|, \aleph _0\} $, see <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Ch. I, 10.13</span>, <a href="/bibliography/Kunen">Kunen</a>]</span>. On the other hand, we see that $\Gamma (U, \mathcal{O}) \subset R_{f_1} \times \ldots \times R_{f_ n}$ and hence $|\Gamma (U, \mathcal{O})| \leq \max \{ \aleph _0, |R_{f_1}|, \ldots , |R_{f_ n}|\} $. Thus it suffices to prove that $|R_ f| \leq \max \{ \aleph _0, |R|\} $ which is omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>